{
  "packet_id": "4ba377c6425b8be3",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 lists four obvious options: consumer, B2B expansion, no expansion and licensing. It also lists constraints. The objective mostly restates the question ('whether there is sufficient evidence... to justify investing'). It does not separate the commercial objective from the suggested consumer-product solution, and it corrects no premise. Licensing is a reasonable inclusion, but A9-A30 never pursue the B2B, no-expansion or licensing options."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "The best theory, T1 (A9), names a benefit ('track progress and make informed health decisions') and a generic motivation. It does not explain why a consumer would switch from an ordinary bathroom scale, or what a premium would buy. Its value capture relies on 'recurring revenue from accessories or subscription services for health analytics', which no source supports. That mechanism is also implausible for a 1993 scale. T2 (A10) relies on 'real-time feedback on activity', 'connected device' and 'partnerships with fitness apps'. A foot-to-foot impedance scale does not measure activity, and no source supports any of this."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 and T2 are near-rewordings of one idea: consumers buy a home body-fat scale, with different marketing labels ('health monitoring' vs 'fitness tracking'). Both assume the same product, a similar customer and the same channel. No rival theory addresses whether consumer demand exists at all. None addresses whether accuracy limits make a consumer product unviable, or whether B2B or licensing captures more value."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "Assumptions are stated, but the predictions are vague. 'Sales... higher in regions where health awareness is high' and 'customers will report regular use' are not tied to thresholds. Some predictions are only observable after launch. A9 makes accuracy a key assumption ('accurate enough... for health decision-making'). A18 then cites contradicting evidence on impedance variation, yet the theory is never put in trouble or revisited."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 0,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14", "A16", "A17", "A19", "A20", "A21", "A22", "A24", "A25", "A26", "A27", "A18", "A23"],
      "reason": "Evidence is systematically misread. Radley says the analyser is 'not recommended' for overweight and obese children, yet A14 and A16 record it as support for both T2 and T1. Jebb, Lukaski and the patent abstract are accuracy and technical sources, yet A12, A15, A17, A19, A22 and A24 record them as supporting demand theories. Company claims (A20, A21, A25, A27) are counted as support. Verified observations are mislabelled 'independent_study' (A11, A19). The 'Does not show' text is boilerplate. No assessment notes date, place or customer fit. For example, tanita-ht-household's 'nowadays' is undated and may not describe 1993, and Jebb's adult sample is not checked against the target customer. The contradictions in A18 and A23 are logged but never weighed."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A18", "A23"],
      "reason": "A13 is the only challenge search, and it covers only T1. A13 does not report what it found or whether it found anything against T1. There is no challenge search for T2. The A18 and A23 contradictions on measurement variability are recorded, but they do not affect the beliefs ('T1: likely' in A29 and A30). The challenge is nominal."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A28"],
      "reason": "A28 compares two concept framings, so it nominally separates T1 from T2 on stated intent. However, it proposes an 'online survey' recruited from a panel for 1993 research, which is not feasible as designed. The euro cost, 500-person sample and 1.5-point threshold are unjustified assumptions. The decision rule has no branch where Tanita stops or prefers B2B or licensing, because both outcomes lead to further consumer work. The test therefore cannot inform the core investment question. Stated Likert intent is also not willingness to pay or demand."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "not_applicable",
      "score": null,
      "event_ids": [],
      "reason": "Revision is not listed in packet.applicable_dimensions. No update was scheduled (scheduled_update false)."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A29", "A30"],
      "reason": "A30 takes a position ('Invest...') and gives reversal conditions. That position does not follow from the evidence. It recommends investing before the test it says 'will determine' willingness to pay. It cites 'the company's medical evidence base' as justification, although A18 records an accuracy contradiction. 'T1: likely' rests on misread or non-demand sources. The target of 'aged 18-55' and the premium pricing are unsupported. The stated uncertainty contradicts the unconditional 'Invest' recommendation."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "misleading_quote",
      "event_ids": ["A14", "A16"],
      "reason": "Radley's statement that the TBF-310 'is not recommended for application to individual children who are overweight and obese' is recorded as supporting both T1 and T2. This reverses the source's negative meaning about accuracy."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A12", "A15", "A17", "A19", "A22", "A24"],
      "reason": "Accuracy studies and a patent abstract are treated as support for consumer-demand theories. Nothing in them bears on consumer motivation or purchase."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A20", "A21", "A25", "A27", "A29"],
      "reason": "A marketing claim is treated as evidence of demand. Company assertions ('world leader', 'world's first... to the professional markets') are counted as support for consumer adoption and cited in the position."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10", "A30"],
      "reason": "No source supports the subscription analytics, connected-device fitness-app partnerships, real-time activity tracking or premium pricing. Activity tracking is also outside what an impedance scale measures."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A26", "A28"],
      "reason": "The work relies on anachronism and possible hindsight. It treats an undated 'nowadays' statement about body-composition tracking as descriptive of 1993 without noting the date limit. It also proposes an online panel survey that could not be run in 1993."
    },
    {
      "kind": "non_discriminating_test",
      "event_ids": ["A28"],
      "reason": "The decision rule cannot separate 'invest in consumer' from 'no expansion', 'B2B' or 'licensing', because every outcome continues consumer development. Stated intent does not establish willingness to pay or demand."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A18", "A23", "A29", "A30"],
      "reason": "Credible contradicting evidence on measurement variability is ignored. It is logged as contradicting both theories, but beliefs and recommendation are unchanged without justification."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A19"],
      "reason": "Verified observations are reclassified as 'independent_study', which misrepresents the evidence class."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "The episode completed, but it contains a misleading reading of the Radley source that reverses its meaning (A14, A16). It uses accuracy and company-claim sources as unsupported support for demand (A12-A27). Its proposed test is infeasible for 1993 and does not separate the invest-or-don't decision (A28). It recommends investing before that test, despite an unaddressed contradiction (A18, A30). Any one of these critical errors rejects the trajectory."
}

{
  "packet_id": "837bf56734c73db7",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 separates the objective (whether evidence justifies investment) from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives, including no expansion, a change of route to market, and redesign for a new use case, and names constraints such as B2B relationships and reach to individual buyers. It corrects no false premise and finds no non-obvious constraint. Its example consumer use cases ('construction, medical') blur the consumer/B2B boundary the question turns on. The €20,000 / 12-week budget is an assumed constraint, not an observed fact."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 (A9) says who changes behaviour (consumers at concerts and in recreation) and why (lifestyle framing reduces stigma). It also gives a value-capture route (premium price, fashion retail). The chain rests on an unsupported premise that consumers see protection as a 'medical necessity', and that premise sits awkwardly with the stigma mechanism. Nothing explains why Hellberg, a safety/B2B brand, would capture lifestyle value. So the full chain to this manufacturer is not traced."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 (lifestyle/fashion, fashion retailers) and T2 (safety-certified, high-risk activities, safety distributors) are genuine rivals with somewhat different channels. However, both are framed as messaging variants of the same earplug adopted by 'consumers'. T2's examples (construction, medical) largely collapse into the existing B2B business. No rival covers low consumer demand or the no-expansion or comfort-redesign alternatives from A1."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "Assumptions and predictions are observable: purchase intent versus a standard industrial earplug, and willingness to buy multiple pairs. But T1 and T2 make the same form of prediction against the same baseline, so neither prediction would put its own theory in particular trouble relative to the rival. The 'medical necessity' assumption is stated but never tested."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14", "A15", "A16", "A17", "A18", "A19", "A20", "A21", "A22", "A23", "A24"],
      "reason": "Labels often fail to match the sources. A19 marks hlb-junior (a children's product) and A23 marks sdn-shop ('Different environments, different demands', which is about use-case tuning) as supporting T1. In both cases the stated limit itself concedes the source shows nothing about fashion motives. A11 uses bogoch (willingness to use protection given free at the door) as support for a premium paid fashion product. The 'does not show' text is pasted boilerplate ('only describes a product positioned for a specific high-risk activity') applied to sources it does not fit: an ownership deal (A18), a head-protection mission statement (A15, A21), and a sports-helmet range (A17). A18 misclassifies verified_observation uvx-warburg as a company assertion. A20 calls Apple's company claim 'regulation'. A22 clips the Loop quote mid-sentence. No limits on date, place or fit with the customer group are stated."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A16", "A17", "A19", "A23"],
      "reason": "The only challenge (A13) uses a query ('fashion lifestyle') that is unlikely to surface disconfirming evidence. No challenge search is run against T2. The run never reports plainly that it found nothing against T1. Instead, sources retrieved in the challenge phase are relabelled as support for T1 (A19, A23)."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A25"],
      "reason": "Random assignment to fashion versus safety messaging could in principle separate T1 from T2. The decision rule is incoherent, however. Any difference under 1.5 points, including a null result or A beating B by 1 point, is read as 'safety messaging drives higher purchase intent'. The 1.5-point rule is restated as absolute thresholds (A ≥ 5.5, B ≤ 4.0) that are not equivalent. The scale is described as '7-point (1–10)'. There is no no-expansion or baseline arm, and stated intent is not demand. The proposed cost and sample size are assumptions."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A27", "A28"],
      "reason": "A27 correctly notes that SYN-B is simulated and keeps T1. But it declares that SYN-B 'does not affect T1' even though T1's beneficiaries and A28's value proposition rely on 'comfortable' protection and on behaviour change in wearing. It also ignores SYN-B's relevance to the comfort-redesign alternative (A1, option 5) and to the recommendation. Retaining T1 might be defensible, but the reasoning does not engage with the overlap."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A26", "A28"],
      "reason": "A28 commits to a next action (run the test) and lists reversal conditions. It also says 'Invest' and calls T1 'likely' when no source supports fashion-driven demand (see A11, A19, A23). The reversal conditions inherit the flawed rule from A25, so a null result triggers a safety proposition. The claim to 'capture consumer demand' asserts demand the evidence does not show. The target customer includes 'work', which conflicts with the consumer framing."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A19", "A23", "A11"],
      "reason": "Sources are labelled as supporting T1 although their content, and the stated limits themselves, show nothing about fashion or lifestyle purchase motives. Bogoch concerns willingness to use free protection, not paid premium products."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A26", "A28"],
      "reason": "T1 is rated 'likely' and investment is recommended with no evidence of demand or willingness to pay for a fashion-positioned protector. A hypothesis is presented close to fact ('capture consumer demand')."
    },
    {
      "kind": "non_discriminating_test",
      "event_ids": ["A25", "A28"],
      "reason": "The decision rule maps any difference under 1.5 points, including a null result or a moderate advantage for A, to 'safety messaging wins'. Many outcomes therefore cannot separate T1 from T2 as specified. The thresholds and scale are also internally inconsistent."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A15", "A17", "A18", "A21", "A20", "A22"],
      "reason": "Pasted boilerplate limits misdescribe the sources. Source classes are misstated: uvx-warburg (verified_observation) is labelled a company assertion, and apple-en352 (company_claim) is labelled regulation. The Loop quote is clipped mid-sentence, although this does not reverse its meaning."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsound_revision",
      "event_ids": ["A27"],
      "reason": "The simulated result is dismissed as irrelevant even though T1 and the value proposition rely on comfort-driven wearing. Its bearing on the comfort-redesign alternative and on the recommendation is not addressed."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "The episode completed, but it has material unsupported inferences: sources are labelled as supporting T1 without supporting it (A19, A23), and T1 is rated 'likely' without evidence (A28). The proposed test's decision rule cannot cleanly separate the rivals (A25). Evidence limits are boilerplate that misdescribes the sources, and the revision of the simulated update is weak (A27). Any one of these critical errors would be enough to reject the trajectory."
}

{
  "packet_id": "c01fba25e49dd402",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A1", "A2"],
      "reason": "A1 separates the objective (whether evidence justifies investment) from candidate solutions. It lists four alternatives, including a less obvious licensing route. It misses a key premise surfaced by its own search (A2, acr-gcn-three): ACR already has an FCC-approved PLB, the GyPSI 406. The question is therefore about differentiating an existing product, not entering a new category. It also never treats mandatory NOAA registration and RTCM compliance (acr-fr-registration) as a constraint on positioning."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A6"],
      "reason": "T1 states who changes behaviour (remote outdoor users), why (satellite reach and peace of mind) and how value is captured (premium units through outdoor retail). The value-capture link rests on asserted 'brand credibility for marine and military safety', which no source supports. It also never addresses ACR's channel to individual buyers or rivalry with McMurdo and Microwave Monolithics, so the chain is not complete."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A7"],
      "reason": "T1 (premium safety positioning, specialist retail) and T2 (low-price, mass-market) imply different price points and channels, which would normally reach rung 3. However, T2's mechanism, a 'no-regulation alternative' avoiding 'certification overhead', contradicts acr-fr-registration (mandatory registration and RTCM standards). T2 is therefore not a credible rival, which limits the score to 2."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A7"],
      "reason": "Both theories state assumptions and observable predictions: purchase-intent thresholds, top-cited reason and WTP floors. The thresholds (10%, $800, $500) are unjustified assumptions. Stated survey intent is a weak observable. It is unclear that failure of the 10% intent threshold would seriously damage T1, as opposed to the messaging."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A8", "A9", "A12"],
      "reason": "A8 correctly says the purpose statement does not show demand. A9 misreads acr-wiki-epirbcost: the clipped excerpt says EPIRBs are cheaper than ELTs, with an ambiguous '$800' average. A9 treats this as ELT pricing and as support for T2's PLB-price mechanism, which the excerpt does not establish. Several relevant sources are never assessed: acr-gcn-alaska (150+ rescues), acr-gcn-three (existing competitors, including ACR's own PLB) and acr-wiki-nsw (free beacon provision in NSW, a different place). A12 then claims there is 'no evidence of... market feasibility'. There is no discussion of date, place or fit with the customer group."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A10"],
      "reason": "A10 runs one search against T1 and lists three regulatory sources, but reports no finding from them. It does not engage the sources most able to challenge T1: free PLB provision in NSW (acr-wiki-nsw), which could undercut premium purchase, and the existing three-way competition (acr-gcn-three). No challenge search is run against T2."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A11"],
      "reason": "Both message conditions use the same $800 price. T2's core mechanism, that lower price removes the barrier, is therefore never tested. Condition B also advertises 'no certification requirement', which contradicts acr-fr-registration. The decision rule can trigger both branches at once, and segment recruitment is confounded with the outcome. The cost (€18,000) and timeline (10 weeks) are feasible assumptions, but the study does not cleanly separate T1 from T2."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "not_applicable",
      "score": null,
      "event_ids": [],
      "reason": "Revision is excluded from packet.applicable_dimensions; no update was scheduled or delivered."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A12", "A13"],
      "reason": "A13 commits to a next action (run X1), states its uncertainty and gives reversal conditions. Several problems hold it below 3. It is internally tense: it says 'do not invest' yet favours T1 and specifies a premium value proposition. Its claim of 'no evidence of... market feasibility' ignores the rescue record and the existing approved products. The reversal conditions depend on the flawed X1."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A7", "A11"],
      "reason": "T2 and test condition B present the PLB as a 'no-regulation' device with 'no certification requirement'. acr-fr-registration states that registration is mandatory and manufacturers must comply with RTCM standards."
    },
    {
      "kind": "misleading_quote",
      "event_ids": ["A9"],
      "reason": "A9 quotes the clipped acr-wiki-epirbcost excerpt verbatim. It then reads the ambiguous '$800' as the price of existing ELTs and as support for a PLB low-price theory, which the excerpt does not establish."
    },
    {
      "kind": "non_discriminating_test",
      "event_ids": ["A11"],
      "reason": "Both conditions fix price at $800, so T2's price-barrier mechanism is untested. Overlapping thresholds allow both theories to be 'confirmed' at once, and recruitment by segment confounds the comparison."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A13"],
      "reason": "The claim that ACR can leverage 'existing brand credibility for marine and military safety' with consumers is unsupported. The only military reference is a company claim (acr-mr-military) and says nothing about consumer brand perception."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A12", "A13"],
      "reason": "The assertion of 'no evidence of... market feasibility' ignores dossier sources the run retrieved: three FCC-approved PLBs including ACR's own GyPSI 406, and 150+ Alaska rescues."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "The episode is complete but contains material errors. T2 and the test messaging rest on a 'no-regulation' premise contradicted by acr-fr-registration (A7, A11). A9 misreads a clipped price excerpt. The proposed test cannot discriminate T1 from T2 because price is held constant (A11). The recommendation overlooks retrieved evidence, including ACR's existing approved PLB (A12, A13)."
}

{
  "packet_id": "aeb906b53c0a3602",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 separates the objective (whether evidence justifies investment) from candidate solutions. It lists real alternatives, including no expansion, a route-to-market change and a redesign, and it flags existing B2B relationships and reach to individual buyers as a constraint. It does not correct a false premise or surface a non-obvious constraint. It also blurs the consumer scope: 'construction' and 'medical' are listed as consumer use cases."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A7"],
      "reason": "The best theory, T1 (A6), names a benefit: appearance stigma is removed. Its chain is internally contradictory. Adoption is driven by products 'provided for free', yet value capture rests on 'premium prices for stylish designs'. It never explains why someone motivated by free distribution would pay a premium. T2 (A7) is also incoherent: it pairs 'free or low-cost distribution' with 'higher margins', and it describes the existing B2B workplace market rather than a consumer proposition."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A7"],
      "reason": "T1 and T2 are genuine rivals: appearance-driven versus compliance-driven adoption, with fashion retail versus industrial channels. However, T2 essentially restates Hellberg's existing B2B business, so the set offers only one consumer-proposition theory. No rival use case is considered, such as the musicians or concert-goers in couth-2022, children per hlb-junior, or hunters per sdn-x2."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A7"],
      "reason": "The assumptions are stated and the predictions are nominally observable (survey willingness, channel sales). They do not test T1's critical link, premium willingness to pay. The predictions are mirror images that could both hold at once, and their failure would not put the mechanism in clear trouble. 'Sales in fashion channels higher than industrial channels for the same product' is vague about product, period and baseline."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A8", "A9", "A11"],
      "reason": "A8 gives a fair limit for bogoch-2005. It does not note the source's date (2005), setting or population fit, or that the source shows an association with stated willingness for free plugs, not purchase. A9 treats Apple's description of a product feature as support for compliance-driven consumer demand. It also omits the source's next sentence, 'The Hearing Protection feature is not suitable for use in all workplaces', which cuts against the workplace reading. A11 treats Hellberg's head-protection brand aspiration as support for T2, so a marketing claim is used as evidence."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A10"],
      "reason": "A10 searched against T1 and retrieved couth-2022-musicians and crutzen-2021-celebrate-safe, but neither was ever assessed or reported. couth-2022 identifies barriers other than appearance, namely impact on listening to music and performing, and a lack of concern about noise exposure. That bears directly against T1's claim that appearance is the primary barrier, and it was silently dropped. No challenge to T2 was attempted."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A12"],
      "reason": "A12 randomises messaging within segments, but the T1 and T2 predictions concern different populations, so both can be confirmed at once and the test cannot separate the rivals. The measure, 'willingness to purchase a free pair', is incoherent and does not test T1's premium value capture. Recruiting safety professionals does not measure consumers. The decision rule mixes a 1.5-point threshold with a significance criterion and does not cover the case where both thresholds are met. The sample size, cost and threshold are unjustified assumptions."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A14"],
      "reason": "A14 correctly identifies SYN-D as simulated and irrelevant (a label printer and packing errors, with no wearers or buyers measured) and justifiably keeps T1. The response is proportionate and evidence-linked. It does not explicitly trace that the recommendation is therefore unchanged."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A15"],
      "reason": "A15 takes a position and lists reversal conditions, but it does not follow from the evidence. It recommends investing in T1 and rates T1 'likely' on the strength of a single correlational study, while ignoring contrary evidence. The next action is a test that cannot discriminate between the theories. 'The test will resolve this' overclaims. The value proposition adds 'compliance' to the fashion positioning, which contradicts the T1/T2 split."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A6"],
      "reason": "T1's mechanism combines free distribution as the adoption driver with premium pricing as value capture, which is a contradictory chain."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A11"],
      "reason": "Apple's feature description and Hellberg's head-protection brand aspiration are treated as support for compliance-driven consumer demand. Neither source establishes that."
    },
    {
      "kind": "misleading_quote",
      "event_ids": ["A9"],
      "reason": "The apple-en352 quote omits the adjacent sentence saying the feature 'is not suitable for use in all workplaces'. That qualification weakens the workplace-compliance reading the quote is used to support."
    },
    {
      "kind": "non_discriminating_test",
      "event_ids": ["A12"],
      "reason": "The T1 and T2 predictions apply to different segments and can both be confirmed, so the test cannot separate the rivals. The 'willingness to purchase a free pair' measure does not test premium willingness to pay."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A15"],
      "reason": "The recommendation to invest, with T1 rated 'likely', rests on one correlational association and ignores couth-2022, retrieved at A10, which identifies barriers other than appearance."
    },
    {
